From 03364d2e4e01be4a6226478f07a244cba0bb4ce4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 20:26:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] Durability decisions 3 to 6: fail loudly, durable WAL,
 WAL limit, sticky failures

Implements docs/decisions/durability-policy.md (tests follow):
- 3: with durable commits, a commit that cannot be made durable throws
  before it writes a frame: before an engine's first WAL batch, the data
  header the WAL depends on must be on the device and the log file and
  its directory must sync (once per log path per process). A log sync
  that answers "cannot sync" later is a failure; opted-out commits
  degrade as before.
- 4: log syncs no longer wait for the data file: while only the data
  file cannot sync, commits stay durable in the WAL (its header proven),
  the WAL grows and $database reports walKept and walLimit. Past the
  WAL limit (1 GiB, "wal limit" / EngineSettings.WalLimit) a write
  throws before it changes anything until a data sync succeeds.
- 6: a write or sync failure is recorded (file, operation, error, time,
  WAL kept) and stops the engine as before, but the next call reopens it
  read-only: reads keep working, every write throws with the record,
  $database.writeFailure reports it, an explicit transaction the failure
  ended throws at Commit, and a shared connection's later operations open
  read-only. A checkpoint's failure after a successful commit no longer
  throws from that commit (5).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UFnsmor6qyZMSd1VoKwfJh
